{
	"_comment": "Speeds keep a 30:20:18:35:22 ratio, scaled so a Scooter 10 sails Rome-Tunis in about 60 s. range_nm is the longest single leg the model can sail (Scooter 10: 1 min 30 s of sailing, GE 100: 3 min 30 s, Greenline 200: 6 min, Trans Atlantic: Rome to New York, Dominator: 9,000 nm). max_owned is how many of each model the player can own. map_size (length, width in pixels) and map_color are how the ship is drawn on the map: container ships run yellow to red and gas tankers light green to dark blue (smallest to biggest), recovery boats brown (darker = bigger). breakdown_factor scales the chance of a random breakdown (bigger ships are sturdier). fuel_tank is in fuel units; fuel_per_s is burned every second at sea, whatever the speed. Burn was set so fuel cost about a third of a leg's pay at full speed (about 40% since the fuel price rose 20%), and tanks are sized so a ship leaving at 100% maintenance can just finish a range_nm leg (plus 1 s spare) while wearing and slowing on the way. Recovery boats (recovery: true) sail no routes: they recover lost ships, and range_nm is the longest recovery job they can do. carries lists the models a recovery boat can carry (none listed = any). The Mammoth costs the most to run; the Buffalo, the size of a Trans Atlantic, carries only Scooter 10s, GE 100s, Greenline 200s and Coastal tankers and costs about a tenth as much to run. wear_pct_per_min is maintenance lost per minute of running at sea (the Dominator and Mammoth wear at half rate; speed is top speed x maintenance, so a ship at 0% stops); repair_cost_per_pct is set so repairs cost about 10% of what the ship earns sailing. dock_s is how long a ship unloads and loads at each port; repair, then refuel, each take refill_phase_s of that time. category groups the models in the shop: container ships, gas tankers and recovery boats. capacity is in the unit of the model's cargo (containers, tons, head, vehicles); cargo lists the commodities it can carry (data/markets.json). Each tanker size is 10x the cargo and 15% slower than the last. The new lines' fuel, tank and repair numbers follow the same rules as the old ones (fuel about 40% and repairs 10% of what a similar ship earned under the old flat pay, before both were halved). unlock_level is the company level needed to buy the model. seaway: true if the model fits the St. Lawrence Seaway and can sail to Great Lakes ports (Scooter 10, GE 100, Greenline 200, Coastal, and the Buffalo so lake ships can be rescued). slot_every overrides game_config ship_slots' [start, every] every value for one model (the Scooter 10 and GE 100 get a slot every 2 levels). xp_first_level is the ship XP a cargo ship needs for its first level; each level after costs 5% more (game_config ship_level_growth). Bigger models need disproportionately more, so small ships level fastest.",
